title The lever behind the deny list
prompt Take the red spotted earlier -- the per-ship model override that made a tracked declaration false for seven of eight ships while the guard stayed green.
think The fault sat in one sentence. tools/fixtures/d/declared_model.sh calls itself the one reading of which model this clone runs, and its header gave its reason: it opens .claude/settings.json BECAUSE that is the file which actually drives the model Claude Code loads. Both halves were true together for the family's whole life, and came apart at 20260917.180039 when this captain wrote the local file into seven peer trees.
think The measurement was taken across all eight ships before the repair rather than reasoned from one: seven resolve claude-sonnet-5, eight declare claude-opus-5, and declared_model_scan reads verdict=ok on every one. Right about one ship and green about all eight.
think THE HARD CHOICE WAS WHERE TO PUT THE GATE. Making the reading correct and gating it would red seven ships for a state Keaton asked for, which derived-spine already names as a gate somebody turns off. So the declaring roster keeps checking the TRACKED default -- what those pages actually claim about that file, every sentence still true -- and the resolved trio is reported beside it. Gate what a lap can answer; report what it cannot.
think The fallback is per key rather than per file, because that is how Claude Code resolves the pair. A local file naming only effortLevel leaves the model reading the tracked file, and leg 11e proves it.
obs The sharper half is about repair depth. .claude/rules/session-logs.md records this tree's last drift of exactly this kind -- a sentence saying max for eight days while the file read medium -- and the guard seated 20260916 against that recurrence reads the one file that stopped being authoritative. The repair held and its reading never widened, so the class returned through a door the fix left open.
obs THE LEDGER IS FULL, and this is the finding I did not go looking for. construction/REDS.md stood at 63,484 bytes of its 65,536 bound before this row -- 96.9 percent -- so a first draft at 3,518 bytes overflowed it by 1,467. Six trims brought the row to 2,020 and the pin to 65,533, THREE BYTES under. The next ship to book a substantive red meets this wall, and the lawful cure is a fold of closed rows rather than an ever-shorter row. Named here rather than acted on: a fold is its own lap.
obs Two commit-message refusals were correct and both named .claude/settings.local.json as a path that does not exist in this tree -- which is true, since incense is the one ship left on Opus. The wall reads the tree rather than the fleet, and that is the honest reading for a commit body.
